@database Network.guide
@node MAIN "ZZDosBox networking"
@{b}ZZDosBox networking@{ub}

Use Network in the MUI sidebar to configure the selected program. Networking
is experimental and off by default.

Choose: Use

TCP/IP: DOS networking through an emulated NE2000 and libslirp IPv4 NAT

IPX wrapper (UDP): DOSBox-compatible IPX games through an external relay

Both use the Amiga's existing Roadshow connection. Roadshow selects the
physical interface, whether X-Surf100, ZZ9000 Ethernet or another supported
adapter. ZZDosBox does not configure that adapter or change the Amiga's IP.

@{"Requirements" link PAGE0}
@{"TCP/IP: quick setup" link PAGE1}
@{"IPX: quick setup" link PAGE2}
@{"Connection controls" link PAGE3}
@{"Differences from the DOSBox-X guide" link PAGE4}
@{"If it does not connect" link PAGE5}
@endnode
@node PAGE0 "Requirements"
@{b}Requirements@{ub}

- Working Roadshow connection and bsdsocket.library.

- Current MUI launcher and the separate ZZDosBox-Net runtime beside it.

- Embedded DOS, Direct renderer, Normal presentation, scanlines off and
developer trace/memory tools off. Keep core=auto or normal; networking uses
the interpreter and rejects explicit core=dynamic.

- For TCP/IP: the staged Network/ utility drawer beside the launcher,
containing NE2000.COM, DHCP.EXE, PING.EXE, DNSTEST.EXE, HTGET.EXE,
COPYING.TXT and SOURCES.TXT. See utility preparation.

- For IPX: a reachable DOSBox-compatible relay. No NE2000 driver or mTCP is
needed.

@{"Contents" link MAIN}
@endnode
@node PAGE1 "TCP/IP: quick setup"
@{b}TCP/IP: quick setup@{ub}

1. Select a program with a writable C: drawer. Open Network, tick Enable for
this program (experimental) and select TCP/IP.

2. Select DHCP. Keep the defaults below unless the virtual subnet overlaps
your physical LAN.

3. Enter an Upstream DNS resolver reachable through Roadshow, such as your
LAN's DNS server. This is not discovered automatically; blank disables DNS.

4. Choose Save settings, then Open network terminal. The launcher installs
the packet driver and acquires the guest lease automatically.

5. Use Ping with a reachable IPv4 address, then DNS lookup with a hostname.
Launch performs the same network setup before your program.

Setting: Default / meaning

Guest subnet: 10.0.2.0/24, mask 255.255.255.0

NAT gateway: 10.0.2.2  -  the virtual gateway, not the LAN router

DHCP guest address: Starts at 10.0.2.15

Guest DNS proxy: 10.0.2.3, forwarding to the configured Upstream DNS

MTU: 1500; supported range 576-1500

DHCP retries / timeout: 3 attempts / 10 seconds per attempt

The guest has a private address behind the Amiga's connection. Its DHCP
server is libslirp, not your LAN router. No Mac proxy or physical NIC
selection is needed.

For Manual IPv4, use a free guest address such as 10.0.2.42, the same mask
and NAT gateway, and your upstream DNS. The address belongs to the virtual
subnet, not the Amiga's physical LAN. The launcher generates the mTCP
settings; editing the managed C:\ZZNET\MTCP.CFG is unnecessary and changes
are replaced at the next network launch. Custom subnets must be private,
non-overlapping and pass the launcher's address validation.

@{"Contents" link MAIN}
@endnode
@node PAGE2 "IPX: quick setup"
@{b}IPX: quick setup@{ub}

1. On another computer running a compatible DOSBox relay, enter IPXNET
STARTSERVER 19900 and allow incoming UDP on that port.

2. In ZZDosBox Network, enable networking and choose IPX wrapper (UDP).

3. Enter that computer's numeric IPv4 address and UDP port 19900. The field
defaults to 213, so change it to match the relay.

4. Save, then Open network terminal or Launch. Connection is automatic.
Other players must connect to the same relay.

5. Use IPXNET STATUS or IPXNET PING in DOS to inspect the connection.

A relay with no other clients may return no ping replies. This is a UDP
tunnel, not native LAN IPX/SPX, a Novell client or a Windows network driver.
Transport has passed physical tests; multiplayer game compatibility remains
untested. ZZDosBox currently provides the client, not relay hosting.

@{"Contents" link MAIN}
@endnode
@node PAGE3 "Connection controls"
@{b}Connection controls@{ub}

Control: Action

Refresh: Show saved configuration while idle; live packet/error/drop
  counters while running

Renew DHCP: Open a short DOS session to acquire a fresh guest lease; not an
  in-place game-session renewal

Release saved lease: Clear saved guest configuration and disable networking
  for the program; no DHCPRELEASE is transmitted

Disconnect: Close the network DOS session cleanly; Roadshow stays connected

Open log: Read the last diagnostic session's output

In the IPX DOS terminal, IPXNET DISCONNECT disconnects the relay while
keeping DOS open. DHCP and DNS controls apply only to TCP/IP.

@{"Contents" link MAIN}
@endnode
@node PAGE4 "Differences from the DOSBox-X guide"
@{b}Differences from the DOSBox-X guide@{ub}

The DOSBox-X networking guide is useful background: it distinguishes raw
Ethernet access through PCAP from SLIRP NAT and describes NE2000 guest
drivers. Use the ZZDosBox controls above rather than copying its
configuration blocks.

DOSBox-X topic: ZZDosBox support

backend=pcap, realnic, promiscuous mode: Not implemented; no raw Ethernet
  bridge or NIC picker

SLIRP NAT: Implemented through Roadshow; configured in MUI, not [ethernet,
  slirp]

NE2000 resources: Fixed I/O 0x300, IRQ 3, MAC 02:5A:5A:00:00:01; do not copy
  the guide's IRQ 10 examples

Guest packet driver / mTCP: Launcher loads NE2000.COM 0x60 3 0x300 and sets
  up mTCP automatically

IPX: Separate DOSBox-compatible UDP client, selected in MUI

Port forwarding / incoming LAN connections: Not implemented

Windows networking / NetBEUI / Novell clients: Not provided by this port

DOSBox-X .conf network sections: Not accepted by the ZZDosBox configuration
  editor; use Network settings

@{"Contents" link MAIN}
@endnode
@node PAGE5 "If it does not connect"
@{b}If it does not connect@{ub}

- Missing tools: check ZZDosBox-Net and the seven TCP/IP utility files.

- Configuration rejected: check interpreter/Direct/Normal settings, numeric
addresses, matching subnet/gateway and a DNS resolver outside the guest
subnet.

- DHCP fails: inspect Open log for packet-driver or configuration errors;
check that the virtual subnet does not overlap the LAN.

- Lease succeeds but traffic fails: check Roadshow's route; a guest lease
alone does not establish external connectivity.

- IP works but names fail: enter a reachable Upstream DNS and retry DNS
lookup.

- IPX fails: check relay IPv4, matching UDP port, host firewall and a second
connected client. Hostnames are not supported in the relay address field.

Detailed settings and test evidence  Amiga requirements

@{"Contents" link MAIN}
@endnode
